\section{Describe Where Work Happens, Not What ADD Means}

An instruction set architecture (ISA) gives the programmer a state transition: given an instruction and architectural state, what are the new register values, memory effects, and next program counter? A microarchitecture (MiA) explains how a particular implementation realizes that transition while other instructions are in flight. Moving register reads from decode to execute can change the timing of a dependency without changing the sum that an addition must produce. Keeping those two descriptions separate lets the same instruction definition serve several implementations, provided each can carry out all its required operations.

VADL's MiA describes cyclic pipeline stages, their connections, and logic elements such as forwarding or control. Section~3.4 of the paper distinguishes the latency of a stage from its restart interval \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. A multiplier with latency three and restart interval one can accept independent inputs in cycles 1, 2, and 3 and finish them in cycles 4, 5, and 6 under the convention that acceptance occurs at the start of a cycle. A non-pipelined version with restart interval three must wait until cycle 4 to accept its second input. Neither choice settles when a dependent instruction can read the first result: that also depends on where the consumer needs it and what paths can deliver it.

\section{Read the Stage Boundary as a Data Dependency}

The following VADL excerpt is the fetch and decode pair from the paper's Listing~9, with typography normalized. It belongs inside the example MiA implementing RV32I; the ISA supplies the register file \texttt{X}.

\begin{verbatim}
stage FETCH -> (fr: FetchResult) = {
  fr := fetchNext
}
stage DECODE -> (ir: Instruction) = {
  let instr = decode(FETCH.fr) in {
    instr.read(@X)
    ir := instr
  }
}
\end{verbatim}

The reference to \texttt{FETCH.fr}, not the textual order of declarations alone, wires decode to fetch. The generator uses the ISA's encodings to implement \texttt{decode}. The \texttt{Instruction} type hides two things: which instruction was decoded and how much of its behavior has already been performed. Operations such as \texttt{read(@X)} and \texttt{compute} request portions of that behavior; they are not callbacks to execute a whole ISA instruction at once. Referring to \texttt{@X} narrows the mapping to a named resource. That gives the description more control over placement but makes it less reusable with an ISA that lacks a corresponding resource.

In the remaining stages of the example, execute computes arithmetic and deals with the control path, memory maps accesses to \texttt{MEM}, and write-back maps writes to \texttt{X}. For a simple immediate addition, the memory stage can pass the pending register write onward even though it does not perform a data-memory access. The schematic in \Cref{fig:visual-331} shows payload selected for that one instruction; the real pipeline also carries control and validity information and must accommodate other instructions.

