\section{Lower an Instruction by Tracking Its Remaining Work}

Section~4.7.1 combines instruction dataflow in an instruction progress graph (IPG). As the generator walks stage dependencies in topological order, each mapping matches the appropriate nodes whose operands are available or become available within that mapping. Available values are retained across a stage edge only if still needed. The pseudocode shows the idea on a single projected path; it is not VADL syntax or a reproduction of the generator.

\begin{verbatim}
pending = instruction_graph(ADDI)
live = decoded_fields(pending)
for stage in [D, E, M, W]:
    for mapping in stage.mappings:
        while ready_match(mapping, pending, live) as node:
            emit(node, stage)          # available inputs only
            mark_done(node, pending, live)
    live = retain_inputs_needed_by_pending_nodes(live, pending)
    record_cross_stage_payload(stage, live)
require no_unrealized_semantics(pending)
\end{verbatim}

Take \texttt{addi x5, x1, -3} with \(x_1=7\). After D, the later stages still need the value \(7\), the sign-extended immediate, and destination index \(5\). After E they need the sum \(4\) and index \(5\), not the original operands. No operation on data memory matches at M. W consumes both remaining values and emits the write. The invariant is that every remaining operation can get its already-produced inputs from the retained payload; there must be no required operation left after W. If \texttt{compute} is placed before the necessary register read, and no later mapping covers it, the addition remains unrealized and the generator should reject the composition rather than drop the write.

Every cross-stage payload also needs a validity and control interpretation. On a stall it must be held rather than recomputed from a changing upstream instruction; on a flush it must be made unable to commit. Carrying only the arithmetic fields in the diagram would be insufficient for a branch recovery, because a numerically plausible stale payload could otherwise write its destination after it had been squashed.

\section{What a Stage Description Does Not Establish}

The stage graph supplies implementation choices for cycle-model and hardware generation, not a second definition of the instruction. Checking that the IPG was exhausted is a lowering check, not a proof that a stalled or mispredicted instruction cannot write a register. Those properties depend on synthesized control and require testing or proof at that level. The paper illustrates wider dispatch and out-of-order structures in Section~3.4.7 but explicitly excludes those advanced techniques from the described prototype's implemented lowering in Section~4.7.1. The RV32IM pipeline experiments in Section~5.7 establish experience with the reported configurations, not support for every graph the modeling notation can express.
